1021072,ca.proc.d.1934-03-14.2.11.1.4,1934-03-14,16ce500b-a94a-497d-a6d8-216f81209b13,ca.m.3411,Mr. SPEAKER:,Speaker of the Senate,QUESTIONS,PUBLIC AND PRIVATE DEBT,PROPOSAL THAT GOVERNMENT TAKE STEPS TO REDUCE PUBLIC DEBT AND LIMIT EXPANSION OF PRIVATE CREDIT,"I will examine the motion referred to; in the meantime if there is no objection the debate may proceed.

",Conservative (1867-1942),,Pierre Édouard Blondin,http://www.parl.gc.ca/parlinfo/Files/Parliamentarian.aspx?Item=16ce500b-a94a-497d-a6d8-216f81209b13&Language=E&Section=ALL
1021073,ca.proc.d.1934-03-14.2.11.1.5,1934-03-14,b6e2c8ed-5f15-4930-a438-2b12049dfbd7,ca.m.6688,Mr. JEAN (Translation):,,QUESTIONS,PUBLIC AND PRIVATE DEBT,PROPOSAL THAT GOVERNMENT TAKE STEPS TO REDUCE PUBLIC DEBT AND LIMIT EXPANSION OF PRIVATE CREDIT,"Mr. Speaker,
the aim I have in mind in introducing this resolution is twofold:
1.	To respond to the desire often expressed by the public that concrete and forcible measures of economic and social re-establishment be enacted and
2.	To contribute, in my humble way, to the research and carrying out of such measures.
Times are hard. Our social structure is battered on all sides. However, the crisis which has been of a very severe character, for over five years, has had the good effect of making people think. In the research of the causes which brought it on and the means to be taken to escape from its blows, the flaws of our economic system and the defects in our social structure, have been revealed to us. Knowing now these defects the people clamour for immediate redress.
Debts-Speaker's Ruling

Unfortunately the wheels of government do not revolve as rapidly as events. In the mind of our legislators, economic and social solutions are not clearly drawn, consequently, the measures to be taken are not definite.
A number of countries, such as Russia, Italy, Germany and the United States are, at present, separately experimenting on a system which may have practical results in their own field of activities; however, in Canada, we have not reached that stage of evolution. Hitherto, we simply resorted to expedients and urgent remedies most of which proved inefficient. The people realize the powerlessness of the methods followed hitherto, and that is why they clamour, not without clause, for the enforcement of new, and well defined measures, applicable to our national problems.
I do not intend to submit, to-day, a flawless scheme of a nature to entirely satisfy public opinion and redress the whole economic system of this country, were it adopted. I simply wish to, make a psychological and summary analysis of the economic doctrines which prevail throughout Canada; prove that we are, on the whole, following a policy which is contrary to the interests and requirements of the country and suggest some remedies, the future discussion of which will, perhaps, contribute to direct researches towards the realization of a national program of financial redress.
We have in Canada, at present, three well defined schools of thought on economic and social questions.
The first proclaims the efficiency of the capitalist system such as in practice for half a century and whose main objective is concentration of capital aiming at mass production, with a forced and unlimited consumption. It admits the present crisis but seeing that it is world wide, it believes that it was inevitable in Canada. Its motto is optimism and reliance in an improvement of conditions. It believes that, with its vast resources and wealth, our country will be one of the first to readjust itself, as soon as providence thinks proper to check the ills from which the world has been suffering for the last five years.
According to the teachings of this school, we have but to await events, without making any changes or correcting the defects in our social structure, although, at times, resorting to urgent remedies to calm the first symptoms of the crisis.
The second school proclaims the necessity of a radical change in the capitalistic system whose failure has become evident. It places all its hopes of salvation in a fuller interven-

tion of the central authority which should assume all functions and responsibilities.
The means which it suggests consist mainly in the socialization of capital, industry, trade and agriculture. It does not state how all this will work out; however, the hopes of happiness which it visionizes facinates the masses, because with our democratic system, we must make some allowance for the doctrines wffiich flatter these violent impulses of the masses even if they are known to be ill-founded.
Finally a third school contends that that the present capitalist system is not in itself wrong, but that it has become tainted, that the economic conditions have greatly changed owing to the progressive industrialization of the world; that capital abuses of its power and has set aside order by exploiting at will and for its own profit, industry, finance and the entire economic system; that reforms are needed without, however, upsetting the whole social order and that these reforms must aim at protecting the weak and the poor rather than the wealthy classes, which have less need of the public's protection than the other classes of society.
The large majority of the people-

",Liberal,,Joseph Jean,http://www.parl.gc.ca/parlinfo/Files/Parliamentarian.aspx?Item=b6e2c8ed-5f15-4930-a438-2b12049dfbd7&Language=E&Section=ALL
1021074,ca.proc.d.1934-03-14.2.11.1.6,1934-03-14,16ce500b-a94a-497d-a6d8-216f81209b13,ca.m.3411,Mr. SPEAKER:,Speaker of the Senate,QUESTIONS,PUBLIC AND PRIVATE DEBT,PROPOSAL THAT GOVERNMENT TAKE STEPS TO REDUCE PUBLIC DEBT AND LIMIT EXPANSION OF PRIVATE CREDIT,"I have no desire to interrupt the hon. gentleman, but the Minister of Finance (Mr. Rhodes) has raised a point of order as to whether or not this resolution, as worded, can be considered by the house. The resolution reads:
Whereas, the steady increase of the public debt and private debts in Canada constitutes one of the most serious Obstacles to the economical restoration of the country,
Therefore be it resolved that, in the opinion of this house, the government should take immediate steps to reduce the public debt and' limit the expansion of private credit.
I find that on February 13 Mr. Woodsworth, seconded by Mr. Gardiner, moved as follows:
We respectfully regret that Your Excellency's present advisers have not taken such steps as are necessary to deal adequately with unemployment, to reduce the burden imposed by the public debt, and to put the agricultural industry on a basis that will ensure to the farmer a decent standard of living.
A full discussion was had on that resolution, which contains the words ""to reduce the burden imposed by the public debt"" and, since the hon. gentleman's resolution has this reference to the public debt I cannot find that his resolution is in order. The question has been discussed by this house this session, and I therefore hold that the resolution now offered by the hon. gentleman is not in order and cannot be discussed.

Medical Relief-Mr. Howden

",Conservative (1867-1942),,Pierre Édouard Blondin,http://www.parl.gc.ca/parlinfo/Files/Parliamentarian.aspx?Item=16ce500b-a94a-497d-a6d8-216f81209b13&Language=E&Section=ALL
1021075,ca.proc.d.1934-03-14.2.12,1934-03-14,,,,subtopic,QUESTIONS,MEDICAL EXAMINATION AND RELIEF,,,,,,
1021076,ca.proc.d.1934-03-14.2.12.1,1934-03-14,7bbf3d8c-bf7e-4a23-9a8e-a716f6c61d8d,ca.m.5302,Mr. J. P. HOWDEN (St. Boniface) moved:,,QUESTIONS,MEDICAL EXAMINATION AND RELIEF,,"That, in the opinion of this house, the government should take into immediate consideration steps which would (a) provide for periodic medrea 1 examination of the Canadian people; (b) afford medical relief for those suffering from disease.
He said: Mr. Speaker, this is the third time this resolution has come before the house, and I see that even now it does not express the exact thought that was in the mind of the mover. I should like to insert the word ""free"" before the word ""periodic"" in the third line, so that the resolution will read:
That, in the opinion of this house, the government should take into immediate consideration steps which would (a) provide for free periodic medical examination of the Canadian peopleand so on. It has often occurred to me that there is need for a medium of approach between the medical profession and a large number of the citizens of this country. The expense of sickness holds no great terror for those who have money, for they are able to pay the costs. Similarly, sir, those who are indigent and simply canot afford to pay the cost of medical services are provided for by the open wards of the hospitals and by the free outdoor clinics, and it has always been a point of honour with medical men generally to see that the needy were provided with attention in times of sickness. But there is a very large class of citizens in Canada-and this matter has been mentioned before-who have been in the habit of meeting their obligations and maintaining their self-respect. These people prefer to employ their own medical men and pay for their services. Upon this class of people, one which embraces the vast majority of Canadian citizenship, the cost of medical services in times of long protracted sickness is a very serious consideration. There was a time when members of the medical profession designed not to name a fee. Possibly at this time doctors were supposed to possess superior intelligence and almost supernatural powers. For their services they received gifts and gratuities. Those were the early days of medicine, at a time when the field of medicine was limited outside of injuries and maternity cases, to fevers, humors and tumors. The therapeutic application consisted largely in blistering, bleeding and purging. Those therapeutic measures have survived to the present day, and are still effectual. However, since that time the medical profession has made very definite and rapid strides until to-day it has become a definite and intricate
science. To acquire knowledge of medical learning long years of study are required and to keep abreast of the times, continuous study from day to day is necessary. I would express the belief that very few medical men of to-day could afford to take a chance on earning a livelihood through receipts of gratuities and gifts. On the other hand, it is necessary for them to charge fees, and we understand that medical fees have always been fairly high. Speaking of high medical fees I am not referring to those men who charge $25 or $50 for advice. I consider that, like the lawyer who may charge $1,000 for advice, they are parasites of society and one of the fertile causes of communism and revolution.

",Liberal,,John Power Howden,http://www.parl.gc.ca/parlinfo/Files/Parliamentarian.aspx?Item=7bbf3d8c-bf7e-4a23-9a8e-a716f6c61d8d&Language=E&Section=ALL
1021077,ca.proc.d.1934-03-14.2.12.2,1934-03-14,intervention,,,,QUESTIONS,MEDICAL EXAMINATION AND RELIEF,,"Where are they?

",,,An hon. MEMBER:,
1021078,ca.proc.d.1934-03-14.2.12.3,1934-03-14,7bbf3d8c-bf7e-4a23-9a8e-a716f6c61d8d,ca.m.5302,Mr. HOWDEN:,,QUESTIONS,MEDICAL EXAMINATION AND RELIEF,,"I have heard of them.
At this time, however, I am talking about ordinary medical fees running from $2 to $5 for office consultations, and about S3 for household calls. These are the fees commonly charged. I say to hon. members that in a family, the breadwinner of which earns only $2 a day and who is called upon to pay $3 a day for medical services over a considerable period of time, a hardship exists. For that reason I dare say in a great many instances sickness is, at least to some extent, neglected and is allowed to gain considerable headway before a medical man is called in. That, as hon. members will realize, is a very undesirable state of affairs.
I should like to approach the subject from another angle, namely, that sickness is always with us. Of those of us born into this life unfortunately some are born as imbeciles and morons. At the present time I am not giving special consideration to this class. There is however a class scarcely less fortunate, one born into this world with an inferior constitution and subnormal vital resistance, people who go through life as invalids. Then' we have those people who do not know they are invalids, and who have had very little to cause them to suspect any physical abnormality; I refer to those amongst others affected with tubercular trouble. It is estimated that about 80 per cent of humanity is either actively affected with tuberculosis, or have the disease lying latent in their systems.
I fancy it would be safe to say there are many dozens or even hundreds of cases of incipient heart trouble and liver trouble which are not suspected and are not known. The number of people who give positive reaction to the Was-sermann test for syphilis is appalling. That is the only word which could properly be used.
With these considerations in mind I was prompted in the year 1931 to bring before this



Medical Relief-Mr. Howden
house a resolution along similar lines. Although not in exactly the same wording as the present resolution, it had very much the same meaning, and was as follows.
That, in the opinion of this house, a measure of federal state medicine would be m the best interests of the Canadian people.
On that occasion it was my endeavour to point out that the wealth of the world had been gained not by gold but by labour, and that labour could produce only with the aid of sound bodies and sound minds. I indicated that since Canada is a land of stem realities, it required men of sound physical and mental health. It was pointed out that a large share of the population was suffering from illnesses which cost our people many millions of dollars yearly. Through unsuspected illnesses constantly among us the health of many of our numbers was constantly being undermined.
The resolution was discussed at some length by sixteen or seventeen hon. members, most of whom, I believe, seemed favourably impressed with the resolution. On that occasion the Minister of Pensions and National Health said this:
But with respect to the general question, I have sympathy with the idea of medical treatment being provided in some organized way for those who require it. I do not suggest that it come under the dominion government.
I am supporting and I am sympathetic with the idea that there should be a system of medical treatment in this country for those who need it. Whether it shall be provided by the provincial government, or by the federal government, or by a combination of the two, is a different matter.
So that even the Minister of Pensions and National Health realized at that time, as I believe he does to-day that it would be desirable if we could get medical treatment for those desiring and requiring it, who are unable to pay for it but who through personal selfrespect are prevented from asking for it freely.
The hon. member for Medicine Hat (Mr. Gershaw) believed that such a condition would come about in time, but that if conditions were left as they then existed we would, in time, reach the desired haven. The Prime Minister (Mr. Bennett), however, took decided exception to the resolution and, if I remember correctly, pointed out that if adopted it would entail the entire disorganization of the medical profession, and that there would be trouble between the provinces and the dominion in connection with the control of medical services. I think possibly the

right hon. gentleman thought that the cost would be rather excessive. At the time I was inclined to think, although I may have been wrong, that he believed that many of his supporters were ready to give this measure their favourable consideration. However, he gave directions as to what should be done and the resolution was voted down by the government. The following year the matter came up in a rather different form, the same form as it appears to-day, namely:
That, in the opinion of this house, the government should take into immediate consideration steps which would (a) provide for periodic medical examination of the Canadian people; (b) afford medical relief for those suffering from disease.
On that occasion the resolution came up well on in the evening, and there was only time for the Minister of Pensions and National Health (Mr. MaoLaren), the hon. member for East Calgary (Mr. Stanley) and the hon. member for Kingston (Mr. Ross) to respond. I pause to say that the hon. members for East Calgary and Kingston made very excellent addresses. I think I am right in stating that they both agreed with me in that they felt that the services already given the Canadian people could be correlated under federal control and that if that could be done and the provinces brought together in a common standard, it would be a desirable condition. I must not say that the hon. gentlemen agreed with me further. The position taken by the minister was similar to that taken the year before, except that he pointed out there had not been any great demand for periodic medical examination. He stated that those who could afford such examinations or had them available made little use of them. As I said, the motion came on well in the evening and did not receive very much consideration.

",Liberal,,John Power Howden,http://www.parl.gc.ca/parlinfo/Files/Parliamentarian.aspx?Item=7bbf3d8c-bf7e-4a23-9a8e-a716f6c61d8d&Language=E&Section=ALL
1021079,ca.proc.d.1934-03-14.2.12.4,1934-03-14,b9296f13-96f7-4c62-a577-63a5fc91ac2f,ca.m.6164,Mr. BENNETT:,Prime Minister; President of the Privy Council; Secretary of State for External Affairs,QUESTIONS,MEDICAL EXAMINATION AND RELIEF,,"I might say that no
direction was given to the party to the right except such as they may have taken from the observations I made.

",Conservative (1867-1942),,Richard Bedford Bennett,http://www.parl.gc.ca/parlinfo/Files/Parliamentarian.aspx?Item=b9296f13-96f7-4c62-a577-63a5fc91ac2f&Language=E&Section=ALL
1021080,ca.proc.d.1934-03-14.2.12.5,1934-03-14,7bbf3d8c-bf7e-4a23-9a8e-a716f6c61d8d,ca.m.5302,Mr. HOWDEN:,,QUESTIONS,MEDICAL EXAMINATION AND RELIEF,,"We will leave it at that.
I should like to appeal to the Prime Minister to afford if possible an opportunity of completing this discussion. We have not had an opportunity either this year or last year.

",Liberal,,John Power Howden,http://www.parl.gc.ca/parlinfo/Files/Parliamentarian.aspx?Item=7bbf3d8c-bf7e-4a23-9a8e-a716f6c61d8d&Language=E&Section=ALL
1021081,ca.proc.d.1934-03-14.2.12.6,1934-03-14,b9296f13-96f7-4c62-a577-63a5fc91ac2f,ca.m.6164,Mr. BENNETT:,Prime Minister; President of the Privy Council; Secretary of State for External Affairs,QUESTIONS,MEDICAL EXAMINATION AND RELIEF,,"The motion was called
once and passed. I can give no further assurance to the hon. gentleman.

",Conservative (1867-1942),,Richard Bedford Bennett,http://www.parl.gc.ca/parlinfo/Files/Parliamentarian.aspx?Item=b9296f13-96f7-4c62-a577-63a5fc91ac2f&Language=E&Section=ALL
